when we think about the Amazon we often  think about this on the left industrial  scale agriculture on the right as a  massive tract of rainforest managed by  indigenous tribes Brazil has hugely  increased its production of soybeans and  beef these are the issues we often talk  about and I went into the Amazon to  report on them but when you get on the  ground you realize this is also a human  story the Brazilian government has  cracked down on illegal deforestation  but there's still money to be made in  clearing land the region remains rife  with poverty and this can lead to often  violent conflict these environmental  enforcement officers are burning an  illegal camp to the ground across the  road as a patch of deforestation that  they had spotted via satellite  I watched as this man was interrogated  the officers went through his home they  confiscated two unregistered chainsaws  and a gun but it was never clear whether  he was responsible for the deforestation  that took place poverty remains an issue  and people are still moving into the  forest this family told me that gunmen  came and knocked down the walls of their  house these gunmen they said were  employed by a local ranch indigenous  tribes are still fighting to protect  their land and in some cases resettle it  this Siobhan Tate tribe got their land  back but when they did there was little  forest left on it  Brazil has rained industrial-scale  deforestation but pressure on the forest  is only going to increase from roads  ports and dams like this one Belo Monte  which will be the world's third largest  when complete local residents and  indigenous populations are protesting  Belo Monte even today they fear it's  only going to export money and  electricity to the industrialized South  in many ways this is a war over natural  resources and it's not likely to end any  time soon  you
 

